meta {
  name: Preview Excel Import
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/family-trees/{{tree_id}}/excel/import/preview
  body: multipartForm
  auth: inherit
}

params:path {
  tree_id: {{tree_id}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  file: @file(./sample.xlsx)
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.valid: isBoolean
}

docs {
  # Preview Excel Import

  Dry run. Parses the workbook, matches every row against the tree, and reports
  what an import would do without writing anything. Always run this before
  Import Tree Excel.

  Requires permissions `person_create` and `marriage_create`, plus
  `add_persons` access to the tree.

  ## Input (path + `multipart/form-data`)
  | Field | Type | Required | Notes |
  |-------|------|----------|-------|
  | tree_id | uuid | yes (path) | |
  | file | file | yes | `.xlsx` only, non-empty |

  ## Output `200`
  ```json
  {
    "valid": true,
    "persons": [
      {
        "ref": "p1",
        "name": "Ali",
        "gender": "MALE",
        "row_number": 2,
        "already_exists": false,
        "existing_label": null,
        "duplicate_of_ref": null
      }
    ],
    "marriages": [
      {
        "ref": "m1",
        "spouse_a_ref": "p1",
        "spouse_b_ref": "p2",
        "married_at": "2000-01-01",
        "row_number": 2,
        "already_exists": false,
        "warning": null
      }
    ],
    "errors": []
  }
  ```

  `valid` is false when `errors` is non-empty. `already_exists` marks rows the
  tree already holds, `duplicate_of_ref` marks rows repeated inside the file
  itself, and `warning` flags a spouse who already has an active marriage.

  A non-`.xlsx` or empty upload returns `422`.
}
